  <h1>Boiler Broken Down and You're Renting? Who Actually Has to Pay in York</h1>
  <p>We get this call a lot: no heating, no hot water, and the person on the phone isn't sure if they're even allowed to book an engineer, because they rent the place and don't want to end up with a bill that isn't theirs to pay. So before anything else, here's the plain answer.</p>

  <h2>Who's actually responsible, by law</h2>
  <p>In almost every tenancy in England, the landlord is legally responsible for keeping the heating and hot water system in proper working order. That covers the boiler, the pipework, and the radiators. It doesn't matter whose name is on the gas bill or who's living there day to day. If the system that heats the house breaks, fixing it is the landlord's job and the landlord's cost, not something that comes out of a tenant's pocket.</p>
  <p>There are a few exceptions worth checking your tenancy agreement for, particularly on very short lets or lodger arrangements, but for the vast majority of people renting a house or flat in York, this is the rule. If you've accidentally caused the damage yourself, that's a different conversation, but a boiler simply giving up isn't something a tenant is expected to fund.</p>

  <h2>What that actually means when your heating's gone</h2>
  <p>Practically, it means your first call should be to your landlord or letting agent, not straight to us, unless your tenancy agreement says otherwise. They're the ones who should be booking and paying for the <a href="/boiler-repairs-breakdowns/">boiler repair</a>. A lot of landlords and agents already have a trade they use regularly, and plenty of the calls we take for rented properties come from the landlord or agent directly rather than the tenant.</p>
  <p>If you're a tenant and your landlord's told you to go ahead and arrange it yourself, that's fine too, as long as it's clear up front who the invoice is going to. We'll always check that before we start any chargeable work, because the last thing we want is a tenant left holding a bill that was never meant to be theirs.</p>

  <h2>When the landlord's gone quiet</h2>
  <p>This is the part that actually causes the most stress, not the legal side. A radiator going cold is one thing. No heating or no hot water at all in the middle of winter, with a landlord who isn't answering texts, is another. If that's where you are, a few things are worth knowing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Put it in writing (a text or email works) so there's a record of when you reported it and what you were told</li>
    <li>Keep a note of how long you've been without heating or hot water, especially if there's a child, an elderly person, or someone unwell in the house</li>
    <li>If it drags on, your council's housing team or Citizens Advice can tell you what rights you have to push things along</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We can't give legal advice on what to do if a landlord simply won't act, every situation and tenancy's a little different, but we can tell you honestly whether a boiler not working in York is a quick fix or something more serious, which is often the information you need before that conversation with your landlord anyway.</p>

  <h2>What we do when we're called to a rental property</h2>
  <p>We work with landlords and letting agents across York every week, not just owner-occupiers. When we turn up to a rented property, we'll confirm who's authorising the work and who's paying before we touch anything, so there's no awkwardness afterwards for anyone. If it's a <a href="/gas-safety-landlords/">gas safety check or CP12</a> rather than a breakdown, that's always the landlord's legal responsibility to arrange, and we deal with Hayden in our office to get that booked and the paperwork sent across properly. Our full <a href="/advice-centre/cp12-landlord-certificate/">CP12 explainer</a> covers that side of things if you're a landlord trying to work out what you're due for.</p>

  <h2>If your heating's actually off right now</h2>
  <p>Whoever ends up paying, the house still needs sorting. If there's no heat or no hot water in your rented home and it's an emergency plumber in York you're after today, call us and tell us straight away that it's a rental, so we can get the right person on the phone before we book anything in. Call <a href={siteConfig.phoneHref}>{siteConfig.phoneDisplay}</a> or message us on WhatsApp at <a href={`https://wa.me/${siteConfig.whatsappNumberIntl}`}>{siteConfig.whatsappDisplay}</a>, and we'll sort out who needs to say yes before we turn up.</p>
